{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And there was a famine in the land</b> — Behold, Abraham went down to Egypt because of the famine, to sojourn there to keep himself alive in the days of the drought. The Egyptians oppressed him without cause, to take his wife, and HaShem avenged them with great plagues, and brought him out from there with livestock, silver and gold, and Pharaoh also appointed men to escort them away. And He hinted to him that his children would go down to Egypt because of the famine, to sojourn there in the land, and the Egyptians would deal ill with them and take the women from them, as it says (Exodus 1:22), 'and every daughter you shall keep alive,' and HaShem would avenge them with great plagues until He would bring them out with silver and gold and sheep and cattle, very heavy livestock, and they would press them to send them out of the land — nothing fell from all that befell the father that would not occur in the children. This matter was expounded in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 40:6): Rabbi Pinchas said in the name of Rabbi Oshaya, 'The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abraham: Go and prepare the way before your children. And you find that everything written about Abraham is written about his children: about Abraham it is written, 'And there was a famine in the land,' and about Israel it is written (Genesis 45:6), 'for these two years the famine has been in the midst of the land.' And know that Abraham our father sinned a great sin unintentionally, in that he brought his righteous wife into a stumbling block of transgression because of his fear lest they kill him. He should have trusted in HaShem to save him and his wife and all that was his, for in Elohim there is power to help and to save. His departure from the land regarding which he was commanded at the outset, on account of the famine, was also a transgression that he committed, for Elohim in famine will redeem from death. And because of this deed, the exile of his seed in the land of Egypt at the hand of Pharaoh was decreed, in the place of justice — there the wickedness and the sin."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse citation from Exodus 1:22 is tagged to the phrase 'וכל הבת תחיון'; Ramban applies it typologically to the Egyptians taking Israelite women, reading the phrase in light of the midrashic parallel drawn between Abraham's experience and that of his descendants."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:50.792341985Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}